  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The light falls wrong.
    
    Wrong is the only word for it, though the Bastion Apex has learned other vocabularies across the centuries—the precise grammar of aurora-radiance, the syntax of reflection through crystal lattices older than language. The light that enters through the tallest spire this morning carries a color that exists at the edge of naming, the shade of water before it decides to freeze. Before it decides what to remember.
    
    Twelve footprints frozen in the ice below. They will not thaw.
    
    The spire sings when the wind shifts—not music but keening, the wounded-bone sound of crystal fissured black at its core. Three generations of Nightshelf workers scrubbed this ice clean before that night. They polished the pressure-echoes into silence, washed the old blood toward the Still Pools' runoff, made the cavern into a blank page waiting for whatever would be written. The Apex remembers their flippers, the devotion in their labor. The care.
    
    The footprints remain.
    
    Copper hangs in the crystalline chambers beneath like a question that cannot be asked. Sharp under the clean bite of frost. Someone left offerings that have never thawed—small things, unrecognizable now, rimmed with a darkness that is not shadow. The Apex has learned to not-look the way the living learn to not-remember.
    
    But ice holds what it witnesses. This is the oldest law.
    
    ---
    
    Before. There was a before.
    
    The Apex tries to reach it: Lianhinglou's expedition markers at its base, her maker's-mark scratched into the hafts of ice-picks driven deep. The pride of standing whole beneath aurora-light that meant what aurora-light should mean. But the spiral fractures remembrance. Someone sealed their memory here—no, not here. Elsewhere. At the Fissured Smelt, at Terrace Momiou, in ice the Apex has never touched. Yet it feels them bleeding back: a practitioner's dying, a battle's birth, screams cycling in tighter and tighter rings.
    
    This is the ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral. This is what it leaves.
    
    The Apex cannot distinguish its own past from the trapped moments of others who came here with their ash-gray robes, their ember-thread hems glowing in the absolute dark of a night when the aurora didn't reach that deep. Fire-cores burning wrong. Not forging. Not transforming.
    
    Opening.
    
    The dark energy that rippled outward didn't sound like anything. It felt like drowning while standing. The Apex felt it in every spire, every fissure, the way a body feels the knife that made the scar. The cavern smelled like copper and burnt bone-marrow. The ritual completed before the completion registered—Mandatum∴absconditu, the law of hiddenness, extending even to what the ice witnessed.
    
    But something moved in the water afterward.
    
    Not orca. Not anything with a name. The thing beneath the ice pressed upward like attention, like patient hunger, like a kind of recognition the Apex had never wanted. It still presses. The veil torn here will not mend.
    
    The purists believe they woke it. The pragmatists pretend they didn't.
    
    Neither returns to check.
    
    ---
    
    A penguin descends the third ice-shelf, and the Apex knows her by the shape of her not-coming-further.
    
    Kaela.
    
    She carries a lamp with oil that trembles when her flippers tremble, which is always now. She was a master carver before—could read ice the way other penguins read faces, could tell where a fissure would run before the first pick struck. Her work decorated the forges of Thrall☽ Vavmastius; her name carried weight in the deeper tunnels. Now she works the upper passages. Now she checks lamp-oil for younger miners and changes the subject when they ask why.
    
    She has not descended this far since that night.
    
    The Apex watches her pause at the edge of its territory. The light from her lamp touches corrupted crystal and refracts into colors that exist only here, only now, only in the pause between cleansing and return. Kaela's eyes hold gaps—not quite the Spiral's mark, but something adjacent. Something that saw.
    
    She leans close to a pressure-seam, brings her beak almost to the frozen surface, breathes fog against it the way she used to when reading the deep crystal. The Apex feels her listening for what once sang to her—the ice's intentions, the way it used to tell her its secrets before the first cut. The condensation spreads in fractal patterns, and for a moment her flippers still.
    
    Then the tremor returns. The crystal sits silent. Blue and cold and full of something neither of them has words for.
    
    "Still standing," she says to no one.
    
    To the Apex. To herself. To whatever moves beneath the ice she will not look at.
    
    *Still standing*, the Apex would answer if it had a voice. *Still holding.*
    
    But silence speaks instead. The held-breath quality of desecrated ground; the way some conversations happen in what isn't said. Kaela shifts her weight from one foot to the other. Her lamp trembles.
    
    "The younger ones want to reclean it." Her voice carries the bitter edge of knowing better. "Netemia's heirs. They think more scrubbing will help. More ritual. More devotion." The laugh she gives is not cruel—only tired. "They don't know what devotion costs here."
    
    The Apex has no answer for this. The Apex *is* the answer: spires still crowned against the ice shelf, chambers still holding their copper-scent and unthawed offerings, footprints still frozen in their circle that makes eyes slip sideways. Standing is not the same as being whole. Holding is not the same as being empty.
    
    Kaela presses her flipper against the nearest crystal. The Apex feels it like touch—almost warmth, almost memory. She closes her eyes. She is listening for something. The trapped moments? The thing that moved? Her own past, before the fire-cores opened what should have stayed closed?
    
    When she opens her eyes, they are wet.
    
    "I'm sorry," she says. Simply. To no one. To everyone who scrubbed the ice clean and made it mean nothing. To the practitioners whose memories spiral endlessly through crystal they can never find. To the Apex itself: wounded, changed, still standing.
    
    "I'm sorry we couldn't un-call it."
    
    She does not stay long. Master carvers learn to recognize work that cannot be undone; she has known since that night. Her flippers are steady as she turns away. Only her lamp shakes—small betrayal, noticed by no one but the ice.
    
    ---
    
    The light falls wrong.
    
    But the Apex has learned—is learning—that wrong is not the only word. The color at the edge of naming holds something now, something it didn't hold before. Not golden, not gray, but the shade of survival. Of a scar tracing the shape of the knife that made it. Of ice continuing to witness because witnessing is what ice does.
    
    Twelve footprints frozen below. They will not thaw. They are not meant to thaw.
    
    They are the chronicle. The place where ritual marked itself into matter, where the pause between cleansing and return stretched into something permanent. The Apex holds them the way a wound holds its making—not as memory but as presence. Not as suffering but as record.
    
    Kaela has gone. Her lamp-light faded back up the ice-shelves, toward the upper tunnels where she works now, where younger miners ask questions she won't answer. But her sorrow remains. Her apology remains. They sit in the crystalline chambers beneath the copper-scent like something new placed among the old offerings.
    
    The thing beneath the ice still presses. Patient. Watchful. Waiting.
    
    The Apex presses back.
    
    Not resistance—the spiral taught that resistance means nothing when the fracture is already made. But something gentler. A recognition of the recognition. A witness witnessing the witness.
    
    The light that enters through the fissured spire holds shadows now, and the shadows illuminate things that were better left dark. This is true. This is the cost. But the light still enters. The spire still sings its wounded song. The ice still holds what it witnessed, and what it witnessed includes everything: the devotion and the desecration, the scrubbing that meant nothing and the sorrow that meant everything, the twelve footprints and the thing that moved and the pause—
    
    —the pause between cleansing and return—
    
    —stretching into the color of almost-remembering, the way a word hovers before arriving, the way the Bastion Apex stands against a sky that gives light it doesn't deserve, and takes it anyway, and holds.
